00:02:58.001 --> 00:03:00.420
So we'll just do
that in this course.

00:03:00.421 --> 00:03:02.985
For us then, data points
are just points in Rd.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.338
There are two broad
things you can look at.

00:03:08.339 --> 00:03:09.677
One is the modeling part.

00:03:09.678 --> 00:03:11.690
I mentioned this, sort of.

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:14.827
Modeling part,
what feature should we use?

00:03:14.828 --> 00:03:17.760
What should be the value
of each feature value?

00:03:17.761 --> 00:03:20.870
So we'll see in a minute
example of representing

00:03:20.871 --> 00:03:22.550
documents by vectors.

00:03:22.551 --> 00:03:25.670
And when you represent documents
by vectors, each component

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:28.770
may be the frequency of
one particular word.

00:03:28.771 --> 00:03:32.070
Component corresponds to
a word and the frequency.

00:03:32.071 --> 00:03:34.190
Maybe frequency is
not the right thing.

00:03:34.191 --> 00:03:36.950
You should take the square root
of the frequency of the log.

00:03:36.951 --> 00:03:39.502
These are the kinds of
details we won't worry about

00:03:39.503 --> 00:03:40.279
here, right?

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.905
But somebody who does the
modeling has to worry about it

00:03:42.906 --> 00:03:43.884
to get a good model.

00:03:43.885 --> 00:03:46.960
So we assume that is their job.

00:03:46.961 --> 00:03:50.870
They have done the modeling, and
given us points in Rd, right?

00:03:50.871 --> 00:03:52.070
That's what we'll do here.

00:03:52.071 --> 00:03:55.190
And the second part
is understanding and

00:03:55.191 --> 00:03:57.460
processing data,
mathematical structure,

00:03:57.461 --> 00:04:00.920
properties of data, and so on,
