00:04:08.551 --> 00:04:09.280
Right at this point,

00:04:09.281 --> 00:04:12.830
I'm going to describe some
examples of modeling, right?

00:04:12.831 --> 00:04:17.300
Okay, so example one, which
is a fairly standard example.

00:04:17.301 --> 00:04:19.870
So if you have
a collection of documents,

00:04:19.871 --> 00:04:21.260
I want to represent them.

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:24.820
Represent each document
as a point in d space,

00:04:24.821 --> 00:04:25.760
where d is large, right?

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:31.330
So let's say the documents
use the vocabulary of d words

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.220
or terms.

00:04:33.221 --> 00:04:36.400
Every word may not make
it to your list of terms.

00:04:36.401 --> 00:04:40.320
I mean, articles may be omitted
then and things like that.

00:04:40.321 --> 00:04:43.400
This kind of pruning the modeler
does, you don't worry about it.

00:04:43.401 --> 00:04:47.875
It's given to us after
all the pruning is done.

00:04:47.876 --> 00:04:53.414
We represent each document as a
d vector, d-dimensional vector,

00:04:53.415 --> 00:04:57.797
which lists the frequency
of each term in a document.

00:04:57.798 --> 00:05:00.372
Or it may be the square root of
the frequency or whatever, but

00:05:00.373 --> 00:05:02.405
it's some function of
the frequency usually.

00:05:05.752 --> 00:05:08.800
Okay, I guess this was
supposed to be an example.

00:05:08.801 --> 00:05:10.780
So here's a random document.

00:05:10.781 --> 00:05:14.329
These words occur with very
high frequency, right?

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:17.970
And so
you know what the document is.

00:05:17.971 --> 00:05:19.808
I mean, one question is right,

00:05:19.809 --> 00:05:22.320
we completely
forgot the context.

00:05:22.321 --> 00:05:25.190
We didn't worry about
which words occur

00:05:25.191 --> 00:05:27.230
next to which words, right?

00:05:27.231 --> 00:05:28.330
So we don't worry about that,

00:05:28.331 --> 00:05:30.580
just call it a bag of
words representation.

00:05:30.581 --> 00:05:33.140
Even though it throws away a lot
of information, it's turned out

00:05:33.141 --> 00:05:36.140
to be quite useful and
used in a lot of applications.

00:05:36.141 --> 00:05:38.230
And if you want to worry
about neighboring words you

00:05:38.231 --> 00:05:41.990
could worry about pairs of
words and their frequencies.

00:05:41.991 --> 00:05:44.575
Pairs of words occurring next
to each other, so bigrams,

00:05:44.576 --> 00:05:46.440
they're called, and
their frequency.

00:05:46.441 --> 00:05:47.920
So you could do
things like that.

00:05:47.921 --> 00:05:52.061
Those are tweaks to the model,
but it's just an example to say

00:05:52.062 --> 00:05:55.260
documents are represented
as vectors, okay?

00:05:55.261 --> 00:05:58.391
One more example,
which also is crucial.

00:05:58.392 --> 00:06:01.200
So if you have
a collection of d URLs,

00:06:01.201 --> 00:06:04.700
if you want to describe
the whole web.

00:06:04.701 --> 00:06:06.260
Let's say all
the URLs are there,

00:06:06.261 --> 00:06:08.750
d of them, the D is very large,
right, billions?

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:14.155
Each URL I'm going to say
becomes a d vector with 0-1

00:06:14.156 --> 00:06:15.850
coordinates, right?

00:06:15.851 --> 00:06:21.780
And the 0-1 is 1 in position i,
if there's a hypertext link.

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:26.120
So one particular URL I'm
looking at has become a vector.

00:06:26.121 --> 00:06:30.925
The vector's i component is 1,
if there's a link

00:06:30.926 --> 00:06:36.175
from that URL to your URL to
the IT URL in the collection,

00:06:36.176 --> 00:06:40.120
0 otherwise, 0-1 vectors, right?

00:06:40.121 --> 00:06:44.990
This potentially could be
d-squared elements, right?

00:06:44.991 --> 00:06:46.660
But of course most
of them are 0.

00:06:46.661 --> 00:06:48.360
Everybody points to,
I don't know,

00:06:48.361 --> 00:06:52.330
at most seven URLs on average,
so this is very sparse.

00:06:52.331 --> 00:06:54.011
There are only
seven d non-zeroes,

00:06:54.012 --> 00:06:55.218
you would only list them.

00:06:55.219 --> 00:06:55.940
But this is

00:06:55.941 --> 00:06:58.460
the kind of detail we don't
worry about at the moment.

00:06:58.461 --> 00:07:02.670
But there are seven d rather
than d-squared ones, twos.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.350
So I suppose most
of this went away.

00:07:06.351 --> 00:07:12.560
So you may ask,
so far I've said,

00:07:12.561 --> 00:07:16.700
take documents or take URLs,
plunk them down as vectors.

00:07:16.701 --> 00:07:19.360
That seems actually
a trivial exercise.

00:07:19.361 --> 00:07:21.460
All I've done is made
a list of things.

00:07:21.461 --> 00:07:23.080
I could have made a list or
a link list or

00:07:23.081 --> 00:07:24.440
any other data structure.

00:07:24.441 --> 00:07:28.540
So you can say, what's the point
of calling them vectors?

00:07:28.541 --> 00:07:30.530
Okay, there turns
out to be a point,

00:07:30.531 --> 00:07:31.670
it's not obvious immediately.

00:07:31.671 --> 00:07:33.470
There turns out to be a point.

00:07:33.471 --> 00:07:37.729
That for instance,
correlation between URLs, so

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:39.616
this is URLs 4 and 5.

00:07:39.617 --> 00:07:45.730
I want to know how many
URLs do they both point to?

00:07:45.731 --> 00:07:48.978
How many common pointers
are there from these URLs?

00:07:48.979 --> 00:07:52.262
As you know, for 0-1 vectors,
that's just a dot product.

00:07:52.263 --> 00:07:57.200
But dot product of vectors turns
out to be more prevalent, more

00:07:57.201 --> 00:08:01.610
useful measure of correlations
between data points.

00:08:01.611 --> 00:08:04.980
So one reason for listing them
as vectors is perhaps you can do

00:08:04.981 --> 00:08:08.615
things like dot product, right,
instead of having a link list or

00:08:08.616 --> 00:08:09.410
something.

00:08:09.411 --> 00:08:13.722
So the vector thing is useful.

00:08:13.723 --> 00:08:17.962
Now not only dot products, but
it turns out our probability and

00:08:17.963 --> 00:08:20.602
everything is quite useful,
angles,

00:08:20.603 --> 00:08:24.044
lineal quantities
Orthogonality is very useful.

00:08:24.045 --> 00:08:27.603
Also principal components which
I will define in a minute and

00:08:27.604 --> 00:08:30.881
will be covered in the second
part of the course will also

00:08:30.882 --> 00:08:34.120
turn out to be very useful
in a lot of applications.

00:08:34.121 --> 00:08:37.211
So this is not just
a bookkeeping exercise to

00:08:37.212 --> 00:08:41.136
put them down as vectors in
sort of linked list or stack or

00:08:41.137 --> 00:08:42.570
like that, right?

00:08:42.571 --> 00:08:44.640
It's not just
a pointless exercise.

00:08:49.895 --> 00:08:52.638
So I'll soon say something
controversial so

00:08:52.639 --> 00:08:54.667
